  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1113 PM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nA few isolated showers are lingering along the WI/UP border and \nacross northern Door Co late this evening. As the forcing driving \nthese showers shifts east overnight expect a return to dry conditions\nto finish out the weekend. Tonight patchy areas of fog will \nlikely develop, mainly across central WI where partial clearing of\na low stratus deck occurs. However, with the increasing cloud \ncover early Sunday don't expect fog to become particularly dense \nor widespread. \n\nNext round of impactful will arrive Monday afternoon/evening and \nsticks around into at least Thursday as a moisture rich low \npressure system lifts out of the Central Plains. Rain becomes \nlikely (+90%) Monday night into Tuesday with the initial surge of \nmoisture and forcing. Then chance/likely PoPs (40-70%) will \ncontinue through Wednesday, before finally taper off Thursday as \nthe system finally exits to our east. Another system lifting north\nat the end of next week could continue to rain chances into \nFriday; however, this is less certain as the models differ in how \nfar west this system makes it.\n\nChances for exceeding 1 inch of rain Monday Night through Tuesday \nhas increased a bit to 70-90% north and west of the Fox Valley with \n40-60% over Fox Valley east to Lake Michigan. The probability for 2 \ninches or more is also a respectable 30-60% across central and north-\ncentral Wisconsin during this same period. This set up appears to \nlook like a soaking rain at times. Some elevated instability and \nimpressive dynamics with the initial WAA surge Monday night into \nTuesday to include some thunder, especially across central and north-\ncentral Wisconsin. \n\nTemperatures will be above normal through the middle of next week\nwith temperatures cooling off behind the large system at the end \nof the week.",
